.TH CBMAP_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_declare_scoped \- declare an ordered map handle that is destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_declare_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro, and attaches the cleanup attribute, which destroys the map on every
exit from the enclosing scope. The macro creates nothing, and the handle
holds NULL until one of the init macros runs.

The macro differs from cbmap_declare(3) in exactly three ways: it
initializes the handle to NULL instead of leaving it uninitialized, it
initializes both companion type variables to NULL, and the handle carries
the cleanup attribute, so an exit from the scope destroys the map that the
handle names at that moment. Because the handle starts as NULL and the
library ignores a NULL handle, a return before an init macro runs is safe,
and nothing in the block reads an indeterminate object.

Use this macro with cbmap_init(3) or one of its variants when the
configuration of the map comes from inside the scope.
cbmap_construct_scoped(3) is the one-step form, for a map whose
configuration is known at the declaration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type. It tells every later macro how to read a key, and tells
         the init macro which order to give the tree
val_t    Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read it; the library
         never gives it to the container
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to declarations and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
An explicit cbmap_destroy(3) before the end of the scope stays correct: it
sets the handle to NULL, and the cleanup at the exit of the scope then does
nothing
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a function
that receives the handle needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return it, and do not store
it in a place that outlives the scope: either action gives out a handle
whose map the cleanup attribute frees at the exit of the scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cbmap_declare_scoped(sorted, int, char *);
if (!load_config()) {
    return -1;             /* Nothing exists yet, so cleanup does nothing */
}
cbmap_init(sorted);
int k = 42;
cbmap_insert(sorted, k, "hello");
return 0;                  /* The cleanup destroys sorted here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_declare (3),
.BR cbmap_construct_scoped (3),
.BR cbmap_init (3),
.BR cbmap_init_full (3),
.BR cbmap_redeclare (3),
.BR cbmap_destroy (3)
